/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Square threads have the highest efficiency but are hard to cut.
   - For a square thread of nominal diameter d and pitch p, the core diameter is dc = d − p and the mean diameter dm = d − p/2.
   - α is the lead (helix) angle.
   - Tr is the torque to raise load W in N·mm.
   - Tl is the torque to lower the load, (Wdm/2)(μ − tan α)/(1 + μ tan α).
   - If φ > α, Tl is positive: a torque must be applied to lower the load, so the screw holds the load by itself.
   - A thrust collar between the rotating screw and the load cup adds friction torque Tc = μc·W·Rm, where Rm = (R₁ + R₂)/2 is the mean collar radius (uniform wear).
   - η is the efficiency; W·l is the work done on the load in one turn; 2πT the work put in.
   - A self-locking square-thread screw has an efficiency below 50 %: putting φ = α in tan α/tan(2α) gives (1 − tan²α)/2, which is under ½.
*/
