/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Springs come as helical compression, helical extension and torsion springs, spiral springs (clocks), leaf springs (vehicles), disc or Belleville springs (high force in little space) and rubber springs.
   - A load W makes each section of wire carry a torque WD/2 and a direct shear W.
   - τ is the largest shear stress, at the inside of the coil, in MPa; W in N; D and d in mm.
   - K is the Wahl factor (1.25 at C = 6, 1.18 at C = 8).
   - δ is the axial deflection in mm; G the shear modulus (about 81 000 MPa for spring steel).
   - A spring much longer than its diameter buckles sideways like a column.
   - A spring has mass as well as stiffness, so it has its own natural frequency.
   - fn = (d/(2πD²Na))·√(G/2ρ)
   - fn is the natural frequency in Hz of a spring held between two plates; d and D in m; G in Pa; ρ the wire density in kg/m³ (7850 for steel).
   - Keep fn at least 15 to 20 times the operating frequency.
*/
